{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on question a019c548, identifiers (c+d): a kind lookup follows\na read, not a value. A scope-typed value needs no genesis read, so the\n192-leaf example needs no budget.\n\nFacts at main df41e599. The runtime check of a scope-typed value\ncompares the `kind` member of the value with the declared kind\n(`ScopeRef` structure, and `field-types.ts:14` for types). The sender\nof the input wrote that member. The check proves nothing about the\nsequence, and it reads nothing. The cutover removes the member. It\ndoes not add a proof at every leaf.\n\nThe construction.\n\n1. After the cutover, a scope-typed value is a SequenceRef. The room\n   checks its structure and reads nothing for it. The declared kind\n   stays in the definition, and the static type rules stay.\n2. The room reads a genesis for a kind only where the judgment already\n   reads or depends on that sequence:\n   a. the sender of a delivery,\n   b. the parent source of a creation,\n   c. the source of each named foreign fact use,\n   d. a membership that a grant or a creation context reads.\n   At each of these sites the verified kind must equal the kind that\n   the site requires or that the field declares. A different kind gets\n   the refusal that a wrong kind gets at that site today.\n3. So the distinct sequences that need a lookup in one input stay\n   under the limits that exist: 128 named uses, plus the sender or the\n   parent source, plus the membership of item 2d. No new limit on\n   typed leaves, and no new union cap.\n4. The genesis read of a source shares the budget of the read that the\n   site already makes from that source: the same deadline, the same\n   concurrency and the same ledger. Where one exact proof read can\n   return the genesis with the fact, use that one read. When the budget\n   or the deadline ends, the input gets today's answer for an\n   unavailable dependency, before judgment. That answer exists today\n   for a fact fetch, so no otherwise-valid input gains a new way to\n   fail outside sites a to d. A budget stop records no entry, changes\n   no kind, gives no authority and keeps no partial cache frame.\n5. Hold the smallest checked value that each consumer needs. A site\n   that needs only the kind keeps the verified kind and the reference\n   after the check, not the body. The operation then owns candidate\n   bodies only while they are in flight. State the resulting ceilings\n   in the implementation note as numbers: calls, in-flight reads,\n   aggregate body bytes and operation-owned bytes for 129 sequences.\n6. The page for definition authors says: a room checks the kind of a\n   scope value when it reads from that scope, not when the value\n   arrives.\n\nIf the core inventory finds a site outside 2a to 2d where a judgment\ndepends on the kind of a sequence that it does not read, stop and name\nthe site. I rule on that site alone.\n\nRuling 79439167 on the cache stands. Its 32 frames bound retention\nonly, as you say.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}